    "69": "The implication is false only if \\(p\\land(\\sim q)\\) is true and \\(p\\Rightarrow(\\sim q)\\) is false. But \\(p\\land(\\sim q)\\) true forces \\(p=T,\\ q=F\\), and then \\(p\\Rightarrow(\\sim q)=\\sim p\\vee\\sim q=T\\). So the statement is never false — it is a tautology.",
    "151": "Since \\(p\\to q\\equiv\\sim p\\vee q\\), take \\(\\Delta=\\vee\\) and \\(\\nabla=\\vee\\): \\((\\sim p\\vee q)\\vee(p\\vee q)=(p\\vee\\sim p)\\vee q\\equiv T\\), a tautology. None of the other three combinations is a tautology, so \\(\\Delta=\\vee,\\ \\nabla=\\vee\\).",
    "178": "Choosing 5 fruits with at least 2 oranges, at least one red apple and at least one white apple gives three cases for the counts \\((R,W,O)\\): \\((1,1,3),(2,1,2),(1,2,2)\\). Total \\(={}^{7}C_{1}\\,{}^{5}C_{1}\\,{}^{8}C_{3}+{}^{7}C_{2}\\,{}^{5}C_{1}\\,{}^{8}C_{2}+{}^{7}C_{1}\\,{}^{5}C_{2}\\,{}^{8}C_{2}=1960+2940+1960=6860\\)."
  },
